WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.189 --> 00:00:02.190
Hello, the internet, I'm Gav.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.023
I'm Dan.

00:00:03.023 --> 00:00:04.680
We're the Slow Mo guys, and we're here

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.490
at Purdue University in Indiana.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:10.359
And I'm terrified of where
we're standing right now.

00:00:10.359 --> 00:00:11.260
It's quite intimidating isn't it?

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.130
You know when you looking
down the barrel of a gun,

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:14.080
this is slightly different.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.870
We're in front of a giant combustion tube.

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:17.880
Yeah, what it's for,

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.680
is actually testing flame acceleration,

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.290
and deflagration to detonation transition.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.170
But, what we're interested
in, as the Slow Mo guys is,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.030
blowing stuff up, so.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.800
Not really, just like obliterating stuff.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.760
Annihilating stuff,
what's the word you like?

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.910
What's the word?

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:34.743
Eviscerate?

00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:35.576
Eviscerate, that's the one.

00:00:35.576 --> 00:00:36.574
Eviscerate is a good word.

00:00:36.574 --> 00:00:38.770
So, we're going to
eviscerate this Mac, and.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.140
Circa 2004.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.070
Something like that .

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.130
When did that I-Mac come out?

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:43.963
I don't know.

00:00:43.963 --> 00:00:45.630
We've got some retro equipment,

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.950
and we're basically going to
put this over here very fast,

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.280
and film it in slow motion.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.240
And we're going to send
it back to the 90s,

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.173
at 28,000 frames per second.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:56.850
How fast does it go?

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:57.683
This?

00:00:57.683 --> 00:00:58.516
No, how fast does this?

00:00:58.516 --> 00:01:01.570
Oh, the flame comes out
about 1,800 meters a second.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:02.450
So!

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:03.283
God!

00:01:03.283 --> 00:01:04.410
Yeah .

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:05.450
I'm going to stand, get the
hell out of the way of it.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.761
I've had enough standing for an afternoon.

00:01:07.761 --> 00:01:08.594
Here we have our Mac,

00:01:08.594 --> 00:01:10.970
speakers and old school
equipment, you know.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.803
On a nice desk, all from sort
of late 90s to early 2000s.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.363
Here, is the combustion tube.

00:01:19.363 --> 00:01:21.393
Look at the state of all these tubes.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:24.870
Do you wonder if I should twist one?

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.500
Don't touch it.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.333
Don't you dare

00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:28.560
I don't know what to do with this thing.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.380
It almost looks like it's
some sort of film weapon,

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.780
where they're like "oh
I've developed this new".

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.116
It's a James Bond, bad
guy weapon for sure.

00:01:37.116 --> 00:01:37.949
Absolutely!

00:01:37.949 --> 00:01:38.840
Man with the golden gun.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.483
And the Mac's gone.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.429
It's an apple remover.

00:01:44.429 --> 00:01:45.650
Yeah .

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:48.570
Okay, so we're going to film
this at 1,000 frames a second

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.440
in 4K, on this bad boy.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.160
And when that, inevitably,
isn't slow enough,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.620
we'll cut to this footage,

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.320
which is good at 28,500 frames per second.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.080
The V2511, cheers Destine.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.350
And then we've got a
light, that's lighting it.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.033
Because it's a pretty grisly day.

00:02:49.424 --> 00:02:50.267
It's blue!

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:51.100
What?

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.400
It blew in the beginning

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.364
That was like, I didn't
see anything in real time.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.880
It did it, like it went bang,

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.033
and the computer was suddenly over there.

00:03:44.458 --> 00:03:45.960
Did not think it would be blue.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:46.793
My table.

00:03:47.781 --> 00:03:48.614
It's blown to smithereens.

00:03:48.614 --> 00:03:51.760
My table is ruined, I can't
believe my table's ruined.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:53.710
Should we see how we have
to use the computer now.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.110
Because previously, we had a keyboard,

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:56.500
and we could use the computer.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:57.333
Now,

00:03:58.520 --> 00:03:59.353
and we just,

00:04:00.205 --> 00:04:01.105
log on.

00:04:03.825 --> 00:04:05.160
No, it's not working.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.050
Well that's 'cause it's not Apple Macs.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:07.883
Oh yeah, obviously.

00:04:07.883 --> 00:04:09.591
Where's the rest of the plastic bits?

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.712
Oh, there's some of it.

00:04:12.712 --> 00:04:13.980
Oh no, the plastic was just
annihilated everywhere.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.330
Look, there's on the floor
there, some behind the tube.

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:19.330
It's just that we de-shelled the Mac,

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:21.390
and then blasted the screen, face first,

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:22.992
into this wall .

00:04:22.992 --> 00:04:24.013
God, oh.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:27.440
Do you want a CD drive, I've got one here?

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:28.273
That's good.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.319
That's the motherboard.

00:04:35.043 --> 00:04:37.630
Oh, now, now, that's
pretty much the only way

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:38.633
to get into a Mac.

00:04:39.694 --> 00:04:40.752
Is to use.

00:04:40.752 --> 00:04:41.593
Do you want to upgrade your ram?

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:44.490
We've turned it into an I'm.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.570
I'm, oh this is all right then.

00:04:51.872 --> 00:04:52.705
Oh, nope.

00:04:52.705 --> 00:04:54.818
Nope, it's not there.

00:04:54.818 --> 00:04:55.651
It's the illusion of being all right.

00:04:55.651 --> 00:04:58.580
So the keyboard is in one piece.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.032
It didn't blow any of the keys out.

00:05:01.032 --> 00:05:01.970
Oh, yeah, it did, space bar.

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:02.820
You always lose the space bar.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.274
Space bar's gone to space.

00:05:04.274 --> 00:05:05.760
Oh and there's the ah, the electronics

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:06.680
of the keyboard.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:07.660
Oh yeah, the electronic part yeah, cool.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.920
So the processes, the pressy tech.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.832
Got your metal in there.

00:05:11.832 --> 00:05:12.783
Look at this.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:16.183
Nothing else up there.

00:05:16.183 --> 00:05:18.140
Oh look, some of this.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:18.990
Oh, the squidgy.

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.650
Oh yeah, the satisfying part.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:21.970
Oh these feel great.

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:23.133
Yeah, a little wet though, aren't they?

00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:24.460
A bit moist?

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:25.553
Ah, they feel good.

00:05:25.553 --> 00:05:26.386
Although, hang on.

00:05:26.386 --> 00:05:27.219
Speaking of moist.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.210
I think my favorite part of that,

00:05:32.144 --> 00:05:32.977
